Step 2 (pick a hypothesis). The hook after the refactor:

function useCatalogResults({ search, level, sort, page }: CatalogFilters) {
  const [results, setResults] = useState<CourseList | null>(null);
  const query = { search, level, sort, page };
  useEffect(() => {
    fetchCourses(query).then(setResults);
  }, [query]);
  return results;
}

The Network panel shows the same URL, with the same four values, about 25 times per second.

Statement: "The effect runs after every render because query is a new object on every render, and React compares dependencies with Object.is, which compares objects by identity, not by their contents."

Step 4 (choose a fix). Proposed change to the same hook:

const query = { search, level, sort, page };
useEffect(() => {
  fetchCourses(query).then(setResults);
  // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps
}, []);

The catalog screen has a search box, a level filter, a sort select and pagination, all passed into this hook.

Statement: "This is a good fix: with an empty dependency array the effect runs once after mount, which stops the loop and is all the catalog needs."

Step 5 (choose a fix). Second symptom in the ticket: "After switching from Beginner to Advanced, the list sometimes shows beginner courses again." Proposed hook:

useEffect(() => {
  let ignore = false;
  fetchCourses({ search, level, sort, page }).then((data) => {
    if (!ignore) setResults(data);
  });
  return () => { ignore = true; };
}, [search, level, sort, page]);

Statement: "This fixes both symptoms: strings and numbers compare equal under Object.is, so the effect only runs when a filter value really changes, and the cleanup makes a late response for the previous filter be ignored."
